    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">clinical study</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGOConceptScheme"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">clinical study</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000028">
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">site</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">site</rdfs:label>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N24db0b31997b42e4ae7981a90828f14d"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N565e592db1c84d8faa3848c074a50740"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://schema.org/Place"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://biolink.github.io/biolink-model/Site"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000029"/>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Location</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Place</skos:altLabel>
    <prov:generatedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-11-24</prov:generatedAtTime>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGOConceptScheme"/>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="http://semanticscience.org/resource/SIO_000019"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q17334923"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N9cb42fee85064eef9bf507b8d4fe398c"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N4c2ba5fbcab14d1aaaea668e09c8db44"/>
    <prov:wasAttributedTo>pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py</prov:wasAttributedTo>
    <dcterms:identifier>PGO-00000028</dcterms:identifier>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A site is a spatial region bounded (in part or in whole) by material entities and may be occupied by material entities.</skos:definition>
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000000"/>
    <prov:endedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-05-13</prov:endedAtTime>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">Semanticscience Integrated Ontology (SIO). Concept SIO_000019. http://semanticscience.org/resource/SIO_000019</dcterms:rights>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1** (2025-12-11) The 2025-12-11 session (jointly covering Site and Study_Site) reviewed five candidate definitions for Site. Birgit Meldal raised a conceptual question: is "site" as a parent concept useful as a metadata determinant, or would it be better to only define the specific subtypes (Study_Site, Clinical_Study_Site, etc.)? The group resolved that the parent concept adds value for hierarchy clarity and future modelling. The BFO formal definition (BFO_0000029) was noted as a logical axiom rather than a prose definition, making it unsuitable as a standalone PGO definition. The GENEPIO/BFO-attributed definition was appreciated for explicitly covering both material and immaterial entities and including body-part examples, but Giovanni Nisato flagged provenance uncertainty (it appears to be an EFO-added definition adopted by GENEPIO, not the actual BFO definition). The OMG Commons definition was well received by the group but could not be formally cited as its URL was not yet resolvable at the time of the meeting (~6-8 weeks pending OMG publication). The SIO definition (SIO_000019) emerged as the slight group preference: clear prose definition, traceable resolvable URI, and no conflicting definition history. Birgit Meldal endorsed it for breadth; Ammar Ammar noted it is "clearer and has a traceable URI." Darko Hric gave a positive assessment. The main concern raised was whether "bounded … by material entities" applies to all site types (e.g. a country's border might not be a material entity). This was noted but not blocking. **Outcome:** Slight preference for SIO_000019 definition recorded. Label `Site` confirmed. **Round 2** (2026-03-12) SG vote confirmed at 2026-03-12 session. No additional substantive changes to the definition. **Outcome:** Vote confirmed. Status → 7a.</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000009">
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">medical condition</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">disease</skos:altLabel>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N127d8e3ce726447c9145c2c9fb5f8a3e"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="Nb3dd672b832d4d7e8438a1a8d07fe531"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="Ne13e86c9ef384fd79049ff51dcff3cd3"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="Nd2f9e12015f44777a936cecd45cb74a1"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N61a8552ca08d442a947fb2c058c74426"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="Nfb7fac222e1f453cb99a91f586ea67e2"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N8da6b31b37a74e1bbd2283dfab02bca7"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">Licence of the definition source: NLM Terms — free, attribution required</dcterms:rights>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">"Source: National Library of Medicine (NLM). MeSH® is a registered trademark of the U.S. National Library of Medicine."</dcterms:rights>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A definite pathologic process with a characteristic set of signs and symptoms. It may affect the whole body or any of its parts, and its etiology, pathology, and prognosis may be known or unknown.</skos:definition>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N338ce8e110d24269b29b5b072b9d9455"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N691648f0447241b2828215fdecd50345"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N4bbfea52dac04afb9b5c24608057020c"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="Nb476b31531a04194957966cf3825aa07"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="Naf227b8e3ccc404190f754209deed2a7"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N7e7885dda8f347499aaafa994a1ef2db"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N6ed992a9b88b47b8bb8da1124a2f1617"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="Nf1000a8214034422afc1c56337061ea3"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N74cec2f28cdb4df0b11218b62325d6ff"/>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Disease or Disorder: Any abnormal condition of the body or mind that causes discomfort, dysfunction, or distress to the person affected or those in contact with the person. The term is often used broadly to include injuries, disabilities, syndromes, symptoms, deviant behaviors, and atypical variations of structure and function. [https://evsexplore.semantics.cancer.gov/evsexplore/concept/ncit/C2991]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Disease: A disease is a disposition (i) to undergo pathological processes that (ii) exists in an organism because of one or more disorders in that organism. [https://disease-ontology.org/?id=DOID:4]</skos:editorialNote>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1** (2024-12-04) The session evaluated three candidate definitions for "disease". The NCIT C2991 definition was initially recommended by multiple steering group members, but the expert group raised several concerns: Too generic — appears to encompass "condition" as well as "disease" The NCIT class hierarchy was considered better than other resources (a relevant advantage for integration) The experts favoured the MeSH D004194 definition for its precision: *"A definite pathologic process with a characteristic set of signs and symptoms."* Key recommendations: 1. Adopt MeSH D004194 as the definition of "disease". 2. Add a separate core concept "Condition" to PGO, with a distinct definition from "Disease". Subsequent note (April 2025): "disease" and "condition" are not to be considered material entities ("things") or roles, but rather as "processes". This distinction may be relevant for future LinkML modelling. **Outcome:** Consensus on MeSH D004194. Recommendation to add "Condition" as a Phase 2 concept. Submitted to Steering Group.</dcterms:description>
