  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000004">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">cell</rdfs:label>
    <skos:closeMatch rdf:resource="https://schema.org/AnatomicalStructure"/>
    <skos:closeMatch rdf:resource="http://hl7.org/fhir/BodyStructure"/>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="https://biolink.github.io/biolink-model/Cell"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C12508"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0005623"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7868"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000000"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N97082a4fbecd4544a45224c7196dcb4e"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N037ef531cd92400aa9ec411872c1244c"/>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cell entity</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cell</skos:altLabel>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="Nc4d9c13d06bf4cc59639c96ca56cb59b"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="Ndb449e573f1841099642e0ed314108dd"/>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGOConceptScheme"/>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">"NCI THESAURUS™ is released under CC BY 4.0. Produced by the Enterprise Vocabulary Services group, National Cancer Institute, Maryland, USA."</dcterms:rights>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Cell Type: A cell type is a distinct morphological or functional form of cell. Examples are epithelial, glial etc. [http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0000324]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Cell: A material entity of anatomical origin (part of or deriving from an organism) that has as its parts a maximally connected cell compartment surrounded by a plasma membrane. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000000]</skos:editorialNote>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000000"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">cell</skos:prefLabel>
    <prov:wasAttributedTo>pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py</prov:wasAttributedTo>
    <prov:endedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2025-06-12</prov:endedAtTime>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1** (2024-11-21) The session focused on selecting the most appropriate definition for the core concept "Cell" in PGO, with particular attention to scoping relative to related concepts. Key discussion points: **Concept hierarchy**: "Cell" is the highest-level concept; "cell type" and "cell line" emerged as distinct concepts that should receive separate definitions in future PGO phases. **Multiple candidate definitions deemed acceptable**: All three candidates (NCIT_C12508, CL_0000000, EFO_0000324) were considered satisfactory for specific use cases. **Selection rationale**: NCIT_C12508 was preferred based on: (1) frequency of use in relevant biomedical applications; (2) its ability to anchor a generalisable, foundational biological concept; (3) ability to accommodate sub-concepts through PGO's planned LinkML framework. A mechanism for alternative definitions via LinkML was noted as a feature that would allow ontological flexibility and layered specificity. **Outcome:** Consensus reached on NCIT_C12508. Formal recommendation submitted to the Steering Group.</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://evsexplore.semantics.cancer.gov/evsexplore/concept/ncit/C12508"/>
    <dcterms:identifier>PGO-00000004</dcterms:identifier>
    <prov:generatedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-11-24</prov:generatedAtTime>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The smallest units of living structure capable of independent existence, composed of a membrane-enclosed mass of protoplasm and containing a nucleus or nucleoid.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000014">
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8054"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PR_000000001"/>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Protein: A group of complex organic macromolecules composed of one or more chains (linear polymers) of alpha-L-amino acids linked by peptide bonds and ranging in size from a few thousand to over 1 million Daltons. Proteins are fundamental genetically encoded components of living cells with specific structures and functions dictated by amino acid sequence. [https://evsexplore.semantics.cancer.gov/evsexplore/concept/ncit/C17021]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Protein: A Protein is a nitrogenous organic compound made of large molecules composed of one or more long chains of amino acids. Proteins are an essential part of all living organisms, especially as structural components of body tissues such as muscle, hair, etc., and as enzymes and antibodies. [No URI provided]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Protein: An organic molecular entity that is composed of one or more chains (linear polymers) of alpha-L-amino acids linked by peptide bonds, ranging in size from a few thousand to over 1 million Daltons, and is a genetically encoded component of a living cell with specific structures and functions dictated by its amino acid sequence. [Adapted from NCIT — no distinct URI]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Protein: A biological macromolecule minimally consisting of one polypeptide chain synthesized at the ribosome [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_36080]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">ProteinSubstance: A single substance with a defined sequence of alpha-amino acids connected through peptide bonds [https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/idmp/ontology/ISO/ISO11238-Substances/ProteinSubstance]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Protein: An amino acid chain that is produced de novo by ribosome-mediated translation of a genetically-encoded mRNA, and any derivatives thereof. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PR_000000001]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="http://hl7.org/fhir/substanceprotein"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://schema.org/Protein"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://bioschemas.org/Protein"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="Nfdf42cde733d441c99623fabdf30815f"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGOConceptScheme"/>
    <prov:generatedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-11-24</prov:generatedAtTime>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">protein</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="Nb2247e4e7b4648188e021000c3121964"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="Nba50c8d192be43a9a4d42481846ffe44"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N1a90f074c7984f0688e4b9d3d03e70e5"/>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">HUPO-PSI Molecular Interactions Controlled Vocabulary, released under CC BY 4.0. https://github.com/HUPO-PSI/psi-mi-CV</dcterms:rights>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A linear polymer of amino acids joined by peptide bonds in a specific sequence.</skos:definition>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <dcterms:identifier>PGO-00000014</dcterms:identifier>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MI_0326"/>
    <prov:endedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2025-03-12</prov:endedAtTime>
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000000"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">protein</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasAttributedTo>pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py</prov:wasAttributedTo>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1** (2024-08-01) The first session reviewed multiple definitions of "protein" from various ontological and organisational sources to inform the PGO conceptual alignment. The NCI Thesaurus ID C17021 was initially preferred by three of the funding partners as providing the most broadly applicable general definition. However, the discussion identified several limitations and divergences: 1. **Synthesis route** — a distinction between naturally occurring and synthetically produced proteins. NCIT_C17021 appears to refer to naturally occurring proteins only. 2. **Functional role** — ambiguity over whether proteins should be classified as therapeutic agents (e.g. monoclonal antibodies). 3. **Ontological role** — whether a protein is conceptualised as a substance or as a target entity in biomedical contexts. The IDMP Ontology (IDMP-O) "protein substance" definition was noted but set aside: in IDMP-O, "substance" is a specification, which limits its utility for PGO's broader semantic scope. Adjacent domains (e.g. production) were flagged as relevant. Organisational definitions were also considered: AstraZeneca provided a classical biochemical definition without a URI ("A Protein is a nitrogenous organic compound made of large molecules..."), emphasising structural and enzymatic functions. Roche offered a definition adapted from NCIt, focusing on genetically encoded entities with specific structural and functional properties. The session did not reach final consensus and a follow-up was scheduled. **Outcome:** Further input needed — follow-up session scheduled for 2024-08-15. **Round 2** (2024-08-15) The follow-up session addressed specific issues identified with NCIT_C17021: **Plural semantics**: the definition refers to proteins as a group rather than defining a single instance. **L- vs D-amino acids**: the definition explicitly mentions "alpha-L-amino acids", which excludes synthetic D-amino acid-containing peptides. **Natural protein constraint**: the definition is limited to naturally occurring proteins. The Protein Ontology definition (PR:000000001 — "An amino acid chain that is produced de novo by ribosome-mediated translation of a genetically-encoded mRNA, and any derivatives thereof.") was discussed as an alternative but also found problematic for its focus on the natural synthetic route. The expert group converged on a preference for definitions that describe the intrinsic, substance-based identity of proteins — rather than definitions constrained by method of synthesis or occurrence. AstraZeneca proposed the Molecular Interactions controlled vocabulary definition: *"A linear polymer of amino acids joined by peptide bonds in a specific sequence."* (MI_0326 — http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MI_0326). This definition was noted for its generality, broad expert support, and compatibility with IDMP-O. **Outcome:** Consensus reached on MI_0326 as the preferred definition. Formal recommendation submitted to the Steering Group.</dcterms:description>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000023">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mirapie#Instrument"/>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">instrument: Description of the instrument or the mass spectrometer. [EBI OLS MS_1000463]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">instrument: machines or tools used in laboratory experiments [http://jermontology.org/ontology/JERMOntology#Instrument]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">instrument: A material entity that is designed to perform a function in a scientific investigation, but is not a reagent. [https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/VDOT]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">device: A device is an artifact that is designed to perform a function primarily by means of its mechanical or electrical nature. [http://purl.allotrope.org/ontologies/equipment#AFE_0000354]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">instrument: An instrument is a device which provides a mechanical or electronic function. [http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0000548]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000000"/>
    <prov:wasAttributedTo>pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py</prov:wasAttributedTo>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N6d2607dc655e401eb8ff3a6ac489b1d5"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N0358619ae671482982b002330e21b232"/>
    <prov:generatedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-11-24</prov:generatedAtTime>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">instrument</skos:prefLabel>
    <dcterms:identifier>PGO-00000023</dcterms:identifier>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">instrument</rdfs:label>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N5816e6640bd347d092ceab2596065f68"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N1d911654a349431eb1f290c516c7d4e8"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N1981d310d1334915904168d2428dbd24"/>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">MIRAPIE ontology. Concept: Instrument. https://w3id.org/mirapie#Instrument</dcterms:rights>
    <prov:endedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-03-03</prov:endedAtTime>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Measurement device</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Scientific instrument</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Analytical instrument</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Measuring tool that creates data points, e.g., thermometer and other sensors or wearable devices.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1** (2025-10-30) The 2025-10-30 session (jointly covering Instrument and Production_Equipment) established the key organising principle for Instrument: instruments "create data." This became the central discriminator against the broader Device concept. Multiple candidate definitions were reviewed. Definitions from EFO, Allotrope, OBI, JERM, and MODSCI were rejected for being either too broad (equating instrument with any device) or too domain-specific (limited to laboratory or mass spectrometry contexts). The SCO/VSTOI definition — "a device or mechanism that is used to acquire attribute values of entities of interest" — was noted as directionally correct by (Darko Hric) but not ultimately preferred. The MIRAPIE definition — "Measuring tool that creates data points, e.g., thermometer and other sensors or wearable devices" — was selected as the closest match to the group's intended scope: it focuses on data creation without over-specifying the how, is general enough to cover lab instruments, sensors, wearables, and survey instruments, and provides concrete examples without being restrictive. The SG confirmed in their vote that the scope explicitly includes questionnaires and survey instruments, not only physical laboratory instruments. **Outcome:** Consensus reached on the MIRAPIE definition. Label `Instrument` confirmed.</dcterms:description>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGOConceptScheme"/>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mirapie#Instrument"/>
  </rdf:Description>
