  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000015">
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N1bdb3ebeea6047fdafb31b684efbf6d9"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N12fd579852cb4e34b3dc62ec13f67a36"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N4343d8b25bc644c3b809ac54c6b44ecc"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N9d807226e54e4d34aa22425c1e817279"/>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://purl.uniprot.org/html/index-en.html#Taxon"/>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Taxon: A set of organisms asserted to represent a natural cohesive biological unit. [https://schema.org/Taxon]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Species: A species is a population of organisms in which any two individuals of the appropriate sexes or mating types can produce fertile offspring, typically by sexual reproduction. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Species]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Species: A group of organisms that differ from all other groups of organisms and that are capable of breeding and producing fertile offspring. [https://evsexplore.semantics.cancer.gov/evsexplore/concept/ncit/C45293]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Species: Taxonomic group of organisms that differ from all other groups of organisms and that are capable of breeding and producing fertile offspring. [https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/idmp/ontology/ISO/ISO11238-Substances/Species]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Species: A type of taxonomic rank qualifying a living entity. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_species]</skos:editorialNote>
    <dcterms:identifier>PGO-00000015</dcterms:identifier>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">species</rdfs:label>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Taxon"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <prov:wasAttributedTo>pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py</prov:wasAttributedTo>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000000"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An element of a taxonomy for classifying life forms.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1** (2024-08-29) The session reviewed candidate definitions and immediately surfaced three key issues: 1. "Capable of breeding and producing fertile offspring" — unnecessarily restrictive; excludes asexual reproducers. 2. "Living" — potentially excludes viruses, which are not universally classified as living organisms. 3. **Virus inclusion is a must** — given the importance of viruses in pharma (vaccines, viral vector therapies). The group requested a community email consultation to gather input on which reference definition could include "virus" in "species". Requests sent to all PGO experts on 2024-08-30. **Outcome:** Email consultation initiated. Follow-up session scheduled. **Round 2** (2024-09-11) The session focused on selecting the most pragmatic definition accommodating viruses. Key findings: The virus/species issue has not been resolved across the field. NCBI taxonomy (the global authority) defines species loosely and excludes virus by definition, although including virus within its subclasses. The group considered adopting NCBI Taxon vocabulary while noting this problem. The original NCBITaxon_species definition link was not found — NCBI does not formally define "species". UniProt Taxon definition (*"An element of a taxonomy for classifying life forms."*) was selected as: Short and practical. Does not include the breeding criterion. Does not explicitly exclude virus (interpretation of "life forms" can accommodate viruses pragmatically). CC BY 4.0 license. The group recommended that "virus" be explicitly acknowledged as considered part of the definition, even if the philosophical question about virus as a "life form" remains open. **Outcome:** Consensus on UniProt Taxon definition. Virus inclusion acknowledged pragmatically. Recommendation submitted to Steering Group.</dcterms:description>
    <prov:endedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2025-06-12</prov:endedAtTime>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGOConceptScheme"/>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">"UniProt data is made available under a Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY 4.0) License. © UniProt Consortium."</dcterms:rights>
    <prov:generatedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-11-24</prov:generatedAtTime>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">organism</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">species</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
