  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000030">
    <prov:wasAttributedTo>pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py</prov:wasAttributedTo>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Subject: An entity, either biologic or otherwise, of interest in an investigation. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C79916]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">subject: The universal for which we are capturing data for or about. It defines the entity for which we want to relate captured content to (e.g. a car, a person, an animal). Note: a subject is not disjoint with material entity or immaterial entity. [http://purl.org/dco/v1#subject]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Synonym 'Study participant': human-centred contexts; note: "participant" implies agency; ontologically "subject" is more precise</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">subject: A living individual about whom a research investigator obtains data through 1) intervention or interaction with the individual, or 2) identifiable private information. [https://hadatac.org/ont/chear/#Subject]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Synonym 'Trial subject': clinical context — use Clinical_Study_Subject</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Study-subject: A study subject is an individual that is the subject of the study. [http://semanticscience.org/resource/SIO_000399]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Subject: An entity of interest, either biological or otherwise. EXAMPLE(S): A human being who might be of interest because they are on a study, a sheep who might have experienced an adverse event, or a pacemaker that failed. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SLSO_0000034]</skos:editorialNote>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="Nd846f336cac74425bf362b4e29846f1f"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N90b153a6a0734a828090a4f0a6831955"/>
