    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="Neafd987da0ae4f799f2ff80ad77db87a"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N072b7423ff82457eb0687dd070f99217"/>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGOConceptScheme"/>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/HL7/C1550501"/>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Trial subject</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Research subject</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Study participant</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Test subject</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The principle target on which the action happens. Examples: The patient in physical examination, a specimen in a lab observation. May also be a patient's family member (teaching) or a device or room (cleaning, disinfecting, housekeeping).</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">study subject</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">study subject</rdfs:label>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="http://hl7.org/fhir/ResearchSubject"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://bioschemas.org/BioChemEntity"/>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/"/>
    <prov:generatedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-11-24</prov:generatedAtTime>
    <prov:endedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2025-09-24</prov:endedAtTime>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1 *(Phase 1 session)*** (2025-03-12) The Phase 1 alignment evaluated definitions and labels for the concept "Subject-Person". The group concluded that "Study Subject" must be sufficiently general to encompass: human participants (clinical trials), animal models, environmental systems (e.g. soil, water, air), microbiome-related samples, and cell cultures. Label "Subject-Person" was rejected as too narrow; "Study_Subject" accepted as the replacement. NCIT_C41189 ("A matter or an individual that is observed, analyzed, examined, investigated, experimented upon, or/and treated in the course of a particular study") was recommended. The group also proposed introducing Clinical_Study_Subject as a separate concept for clinical-domain specificity, adopting NCIT C70668. Data provenance considerations: identifiers for Study_Subject should capture associations with study protocols, samples, datasets, and dates. **Outcome:** Phase 1 recommendation: (1) Label "Study_Subject"; (2) NCIT_C41189; (3) Add Clinical_Study_Subject as separate concept. SG rejected at 2025-06-12 — definition must cover non-human pre-clinical subjects. **Round 2** (2025-08-21) The group tested candidate definitions against concrete examples of study subjects: a dog in a veterinary interventional study; a person in a clinical trial; a rat in a preclinical safety study; an aliquot of compound X in a stability study; a dataset in a study testing analytical workflow performance. Context was emphasised: "Clinical Research Study" narrows to Clinical Study or Clinical Trial. The group discussed whether "subject" should shift to "participant" in clinical contexts (recognising patient agency) but concluded that "Study_Subject" is the more precise ontological term. The broader question of interoperability across pharma functions and knowledge domains was noted. **Outcome:** No final decision. New candidate definitions gathered. Session continued at 2025-09-18. **Round 3** (2025-09-18) The group converged on the HL7 C1550501 definition ("The principle target on which the action happens") as the recommended definition. Philippe Rocca-Serra noted its alignment with an HL7 FHIR resource and that "action" covers both observation and intervention, applicable to any study from physical/chemical characterisation to clinical trials. Birgit Meldal: covers everything. An expert: "action" is too broad and unclear, but accepted the group consensus. The definition's extensibility was seen as a strength: examples include a patient in physical examination, a specimen in a lab observation, a device as subject of maintenance — coverage extends to environmental systems, microbiome, cell cultures relevant to PGO's pre-clinical scope. The group also confirmed: Clinical_Study_Subject (another concept) is to be modelled as a distinct subclass of Study_Subject. **Outcome:** Recommendation to SG: adopt HL7 C1550501 for Study_Subject. Clinical_Study_Subject established as a separate Phase 2 concept.</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">HL7 International. Concept C1550501 (subject). http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/HL7/C1550501</dcterms:rights>
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000000"/>
  </rdf:Description>
