    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Site: (A position in relation to its surroundings) [NCIT_C37901]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Place: Entities that have a somewhat fixed, physical extension. [https://schema.org/Place]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Location: Details and position information for a place where services are provided and resources and participants may be stored, found, contained, or accommodated, and/or where activities may be performed. [https://build.fhir.org/location.html]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Definition source: (MRCT-Ctr formulation)</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Synonym 'Trial site': clinical context</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Study Site: The location at which a study investigator conducts study activities. [CDISC (accessible via NCIT_C80403)]</skos:editorialNote>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">study site</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1** (2025-12-11) The session (2025-12-11) covered Site and Study_Site (both concepts discussed together given their parent-child relationship — Site record: another concept). For Study_Site, the group noted it adds necessary value as a non-clinical complement to Clinical_Study_Site (already in PGO V1.0). An expert confirmed relevance for preclinical settings. The key conceptual insight came from an expert and Christine: study sites must be **approved** — *"you have to go through a process to get approval for a place to actually be a study site."* This "approved" qualifier was seen as fundamental: it distinguishes Study_Site from any arbitrary research location, reflects regulatory reality, and is absent from the NCIT facility-framing and CDISC investigator-framing alternatives. Three main NCIT-sourced definitions were reviewed: 1. NCIT C80403 primary formulation ("A facility in which study/protocol activities are conducted") — noted by Darko (from Phase 1 history) as too restrictive given "facility" scope. 2. CDISC formulation ("The location at which a study investigator conducts study activities") — rejected for requiring a named "study investigator" and being too study-agent-centric. 3. MRCT-Ctr formulation ("An approved location where research study activities take place") — preferred by an expert, an expert, and Christine for "approved" qualifier and broader "research study" (not "clinical study") scope. Giovanni: *"Study site. This goes to light."* **Outcome:** Consensus reached on the NCIT C80403 / MRCT-Ctr formulation as the preferred definition. Label `Study_Site` confirmed.</dcterms:description>
    <prov:endedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-03-03</prov:endedAtTime>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">NCI Thesaurus, National Cancer Institute. Concept C80403. https://evsexplore.semantics.cancer.gov/evsexplore/concept/ncit/C80403</dcterms:rights>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Investigation site</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Trial site</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Research site</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N2efecdb8ffa04e0b813bcf30ec6038c1"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C80403"/>
  </rdf:Description>
